205	<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: PUBLICATION VOLUME ═══════════ -->
206	<div class="section" id="publication-volume-trends">
207	  <h2>1. Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>
208	  <p class="section-obj">Monthly and yearly distribution of publications, accounting for the natural skew toward 2024 due to PubMed indexing lag. Volume trends, seasonality, and acceleration patterns are examined.</p>
209	
210	  <div class="callout amber">
211	    <strong>Data Note:</strong> Approximately 80.3% of records in this dataset are from 2024, reflecting real-world PubMed indexing patterns where older publications accumulate more indexed entries over time. The 2025 and 2026 figures represent partial-year data — 2026 covers only Q1 (Jan–Mar). All cross-year comparisons use normalized metrics. Additionally, 15.4% of records (n=754) have an unknown publication month and are excluded from monthly-level charts.
212	  </div>
213	
214	  <div class="chart-row">
215	    <div class="chart-wrap">
216	      <h3>Publications by Year</h3>
217	      <canvas id="chart-yearly"></canvas>
218	    </div>
219	    <div class="chart-wrap">
220	      <h3>Monthly Publication Volume</h3>
221	      <canvas id="chart-monthly"></canvas>
222	    </div>
223	  </div>
224	
225	  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
226	  <table>
227	    <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
228	    <tr><td>Dominant 2024 volume</td><td>3,924 publications (80.3%) are from 2024. This is consistent with the known indexing lag in PubMed — more recent years have progressively fewer indexed entries.</td></tr>
229	    <tr><td>Q1 2026 partial window</td><td>225 publications in 2026 cover only January–March. Extrapolating linearly, this projects to ~900 publications for full-year 2026, roughly 23% above 2025 levels — but this extrapolation should be treated cautiously given indexing lag effects.</td></tr>
230	    <tr><td>Monthly seasonality</td><td>January shows the highest volume across all three years (1,746 of 4,132 records with known months), potentially reflecting year-end submission surges or journal issue scheduling patterns. Monthly volumes decline through the year in 2024 and 2025.</td></tr>
231	    <tr><td>Unknown-month concentration</td><td>754 records (15.4%) lack month data. These are disproportionately from 2024 (which has higher overall volume) and may represent articles indexed without full date granularity.</td></tr>
232	  </table>
233	</div>
234	
235	<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: GEOGRAPHIC LANDSCAPE ═══════════ -->
236	<div class="section" id="geographic-landscape">
237	  <h2>2. Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
238	  <p class="section-obj">Top contributing countries by publication count, shifts in geographic contributions across the 2024–2026 window, and emerging or declining national research presences.</p>
239	
240	  <div class="chart-row">
241	    <div class="chart-wrap">
242	      <h3>Top 15 Countries by Publication Count</h3>
243	      <canvas id="chart-countries-bar"></canvas>
244	    </div>
245	    <div class="chart-wrap">
246	      <h3>Country Share Shift: 2024 vs 2025</h3>
247	      <canvas id="chart-countries-shift"></canvas>
248	    </div>
249	  </div>
250	
251	  <div class="chart-wrap">
252	    <h3>Open Access Rate by Country (Top 15)</h3>
253	    <canvas id="chart-country-oa"></canvas>
254	  </div>
255	
256	  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
257	  <table>
258	    <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
259	    <tr><td>China–USA dominance</td><td>China (952, 19.5%) and the United States (521 combined, 10.7%) together account for 30.2% of all publications. India is third at 4.0%. The top 10 countries produce 53.7% of output.</td></tr>
260	    <tr><td>Long tail of contributors</td><td>1,116 unique country values appear in the dataset after normalization. Many are represented by single-digit publication counts, reflecting the genuinely global nature of biomedical research.</td></tr>
261	    <tr><td>Normalized 2025 shifts</td><td>When adjusted for the 2024→2025 volume decline, most countries maintain proportional representation. Countries showing relative gains in 2025 include Iran and Japan; however, small absolute numbers in 2025 mean these shifts should be interpreted cautiously.</td></tr>
262	    <tr><td>OA rate variation by country</td><td>Open access rates range from ~42% to ~79% across the top 15 countries. The UK and Canada show the highest OA adoption rates, while China and Japan trend lower — reflecting differing national OA mandates and publishing cultures.</td></tr>
263	    <tr><td>Country name inconsistency</td><td>The raw data contains multiple naming variants (e.g., "USA", "United States", "US"; "PR China", "People's Republic of China", "China"). These were normalized for the analysis above, reducing the effective unique count from the raw 1,126 to 1,116.</td></tr>
264	  </table>
265	</div>
