312	<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: OPEN ACCESS ═══════════ -->
313	<div class="section" id="open-access-patterns">
314	  <h2>4. Open Access Adoption &amp; Journal Landscape</h2>
315	  <p class="section-obj">Proportion of open-access vs. closed-access publications, leading journals by volume, and trends in open-access rates over time, by country, and by research type.</p>
316	
317	  <div class="chart-row">
318	    <div class="chart-wrap">
319	      <h3>Open Access Rate Over Time (Monthly)</h3>
320	      <canvas id="chart-oa-monthly"></canvas>
321	    </div>
322	    <div class="chart-wrap">
323	      <h3>Open Access by Year</h3>
324	      <canvas id="chart-oa-yearly"></canvas>
325	    </div>
326	  </div>
327	
328	  <div class="chart-row">
329	    <div class="chart-wrap">
330	      <h3>Open Access Rate by Research Type</h3>
331	      <canvas id="chart-oa-type"></canvas>
332	    </div>
333	    <div class="chart-wrap">
334	      <h3>Top 20 Journals by Volume</h3>
335	      <canvas id="chart-journals"></canvas>
336	    </div>
337	  </div>
338	
339	  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
340	  <table>
341	    <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
342	    <tr><td>Overall OA rate: 65.1%</td><td>Nearly two-thirds of publications are available via PubMed Central. This is consistent with the broader trend toward open-access publishing driven by funder mandates (e.g., Plan S, NIH Public Access Policy).</td></tr>
343	    <tr><td>Temporal trend: slight upward</td><td>The OA rate rose from 64.3% (2024) to 67.0% (2025) and 71.1% (Q1 2026). The month-over-month trend shows modest but visible upward movement, consistent with gradual OA mandate expansion.</td></tr>
344	    <tr><td>Preprints lead OA adoption</td><td>Preprints show the highest OA rate (~88%), which is expected since preprint servers (bioRxiv, medRxiv) are inherently open-access. Reviews and Journal Articles have comparable OA rates in the 63–68% range.</td></tr>
345	    <tr><td>Journal concentration is moderate</td><td>Scientific Reports leads with 82 publications (1.7%), followed by Heliyon (76, 1.6%) and Cureus (69, 1.4%). No single journal dominates — the top 20 journals collectively account for only ~15% of publications, indicating a highly distributed publishing landscape.</td></tr>
346	    <tr><td>OA rate varies by journal</td><td>Among top journals, OA rates range from 100% (e.g., bioRxiv) to below 20% for some subscription-model journals. OA-native journals (PLOS ONE, Scientific Reports) show near-universal open access.</td></tr>
347	  </table>
348	</div>
