235	<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: GEOGRAPHIC LANDSCAPE ═══════════ -->
236	<div class="section" id="geographic-landscape">
237	  <h2>2. Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
238	  <p class="section-obj">Top contributing countries by publication count, shifts in geographic contributions across the 2024–2026 window, and emerging or declining national research presences.</p>
239	
240	  <div class="chart-row">
241	    <div class="chart-wrap">
242	      <h3>Top 15 Countries by Publication Count</h3>
243	      <canvas id="chart-countries-bar"></canvas>
244	    </div>
245	    <div class="chart-wrap">
246	      <h3>Country Share Shift: 2024 vs 2025</h3>
247	      <canvas id="chart-countries-shift"></canvas>
248	    </div>
249	  </div>
250	
251	  <div class="chart-wrap">
252	    <h3>Open Access Rate by Country (Top 15)</h3>
253	    <canvas id="chart-country-oa"></canvas>
254	  </div>
255	
256	  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
257	  <table>
258	    <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
259	    <tr><td>China–USA dominance</td><td>China (952, 19.5%) and the United States (521 combined, 10.7%) together account for 30.2% of all publications. India is third at 4.0%. The top 10 countries produce 53.7% of output.</td></tr>
260	    <tr><td>Long tail of contributors</td><td>1,116 unique country values appear in the dataset after normalization. Many are represented by single-digit publication counts, reflecting the genuinely global nature of biomedical research.</td></tr>
261	    <tr><td>Normalized 2025 shifts</td><td>When adjusted for the 2024→2025 volume decline, most countries maintain proportional representation. Countries showing relative gains in 2025 include Iran and Japan; however, small absolute numbers in 2025 mean these shifts should be interpreted cautiously.</td></tr>
262	    <tr><td>OA rate variation by country</td><td>Open access rates range from ~42% to ~79% across the top 15 countries. The UK and Canada show the highest OA adoption rates, while China and Japan trend lower — reflecting differing national OA mandates and publishing cultures.</td></tr>
263	    <tr><td>Country name inconsistency</td><td>The raw data contains multiple naming variants (e.g., "USA", "United States", "US"; "PR China", "People's Republic of China", "China"). These were normalized for the analysis above, reducing the effective unique count from the raw 1,126 to 1,116.</td></tr>
264	  </table>
265	</div>
