377	  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
378	  <table>
379	    <tr><th>Observation</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
380	    <tr><td>Journal Articles dominate</td><td>71.6% of publications are classified as Journal Articles (when considering primary type). Reviews (7.5%) and Case Reports (3.8%) are the next most common types. Systematic Reviews and Preprints represent smaller but important categories.</td></tr>
381	    <tr><td>Median 6 authors per paper</td><td>The median author count is 6 (mean 7.1). This is consistent with modern biomedical research norms — collaborative, often multi-institutional. 75% of publications have 9 or fewer authors; the long tail extends to 115.</td></tr>
382	    <tr><td>Collaboration intensity stable</td><td>Median authors per publication has remained stable across 2024–2026 (6.0 each year). Mean authors shows minor fluctuation (7.0 → 7.3 → 6.9), likely attributable to sample composition rather than a real trend.</td></tr>
383	    <tr><td>Country-level variation in team size</td><td>Median author counts range from 5 (India, Iran) to 8 (UK, Germany) across the top 15 countries. Larger teams in Western European and North American countries may reflect more multi-institutional collaboration or different disciplinary norms.</td></tr>
384	    <tr><td>Type-level differences</td><td>Systematic Reviews involve larger teams (median 7–8 authors) compared to Case Reports (median 4–5), consistent with the methodological demands of evidence synthesis requiring broader expertise.</td></tr>
385	  </table>
