388	<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS ═══════════ -->
389	<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
390	  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
391	  <p class="section-obj">Synthesis of the most important findings into actionable insights for research policy, funding prioritization, and institutional strategy.</p>
392	
393	  <ol class="takeaway-list">
394	    <li>
395	      <span class="takeaway-num">1</span>
396	      <div>
397	        <strong>Open Access has crossed the 65% threshold and is still rising.</strong>
398	        <span class="tag tag-policy">Policy</span>
399	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
400	          The OA rate reached 65.1% overall and 71.1% in Q1 2026. This has direct implications for subscription budget modeling, repository infrastructure investment, and compliance with funder OA mandates. Institutions should plan for an OA-majority publishing environment by 2027.
401	        </p>
402	      </div>
403	    </li>
404	    <li>
405	      <span class="takeaway-num">2</span>
406	      <div>
407	        <strong>China's research output leads globally, but OA adoption lags — a strategic gap.</strong>
408	        <span class="tag tag-funding">Funding</span>
409	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
410	          China produces 19.5% of indexed output but has one of the lower OA rates (~60%). As international funders increasingly require OA, this gap represents both a challenge for Chinese researchers and a lever for publishers and policymakers to accelerate OA adoption through targeted mandates.
411	        </p>
412	      </div>
413	    </li>
414	    <li>
415	      <span class="takeaway-num">3</span>
416	      <div>
417	        <strong>Environmental health and biodiversity research show the fastest normalized growth.</strong>
418	        <span class="tag tag-watch">Emerging</span>
419	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
420	          Keywords related to air pollution, animal biodiversity, and environmental monitoring are growing rapidly in normalized rate. This aligns with the One Health framework and suggests funding agencies should anticipate increased grant applications at the intersection of environmental science and biomedicine.
421	        </p>
422	      </div>
423	    </li>
424	    <li>
425	      <span class="takeaway-num">4</span>
426	      <div>
427	        <strong>Pandemic-era research is normalizing — COVID-19 remains #1 but is decelerating.</strong>
428	        <span class="tag tag-funding">Funding</span>
429	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
430	          COVID-19 is still the single largest major topic but its normalized publication rate dropped from 9.4/1K to 6.8/1K. Funding strategists should plan for the transition from emergency COVID funding toward sustained pandemic-preparedness and long-COVID research programs.
431	        </p>
432	      </div>
433	    </li>
434	    <li>
435	      <span class="takeaway-num">5</span>
436	      <div>
437	        <strong>Computational methods are diffusing across fields.</strong>
438	        <span class="tag tag-watch">Emerging</span>
439	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
440	          "Molecular Dynamics Simulation" (+5.0/1K), "Signal Processing, Computer-Assisted" (+6.4/1K), and "Algorithms" (+2.8/1K) are among the fastest-growing keywords. This signals that computational and AI/ML methods are becoming embedded across biomedical research, not siloed in bioinformatics. Investment in computational research infrastructure and training should be a cross-cutting priority.
441	        </p>
442	      </div>
443	    </li>
444	    <li>
445	      <span class="takeaway-num">6</span>
446	      <div>
447	        <strong>Collaboration intensity is stable but geographically patterned.</strong>
448	        <span class="tag tag-policy">Policy</span>
449	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
450	          The median of 6 authors per paper has held steady year-over-year. Western countries show systematically larger teams (median 7–8) than Asian countries (median 5–6). This may affect research impact metrics and should be considered when benchmarking institutional collaboration KPIs.
451	        </p>
452	      </div>
453	    </li>
454	    <li>
455	      <span class="takeaway-num">7</span>
456	      <div>
457	        <strong>The publishing landscape is highly distributed — no single journal dominates.</strong>
458	        <span class="tag tag-policy">Policy</span>
459	        <p style="margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:var(--text-secondary);">
460	          The top 20 journals account for only ~15% of output. This fragmentation has implications for literature surveillance, evidence synthesis, and collection development strategies. Tools and policies designed around a small set of "core journals" will miss the majority of relevant research.
461	        </p>
462	      </div>
463	    </li>
464	  </ol>
465	</div>
